[2026-10-01T07:21:07.890Z] INFO faser.utils_med: A nosebleed could be an emergency if it is associated with signs such as dizziness, pain with breathing, bluish discoloration to lips or fingers, rapid heart rate, difficulty speaking, gasping for breath, fever, or hemoptysis (coughing up blood). Immediate medical attention is required if bleeding continues for more than 30 minutes, especially if the person is on an anti-coagulant drug, or if the individual has a history of frequent nosebleeds. These symptoms could indicate a more serious condition such as a skull fracture or other head injuries.
