 Be carefu l not to cause further injury to the c asualty or exacerbate suspected 
injuries.  
  
Mechanics of injury  
 
Before attempting to move a casualty, it is important to think about the 
‘mechanics of injury’.  This is the process of figuring out what has happened, 
and what injuries are likely to have been sustained by the casualty.  If you 
suspect there is a neck inj ury involved, you must try to get someone to help you 
by keeping the casualty’s head in line with their body at all times, even when 
the casualty is lying still.  Any movement can cause serious, irreparable 
damage.  See page 55 for how to do this, under treatment of spinal injury . 
  
